Greyhollow sits where the mountains meet the sky, a town of stone-and-timber rows cut into the shoulder of a cold peak. The Anvil Father is keeper of both its forges and its mines, and every dwarf in town walks past his temple smoke each morning. Brakka Ironvein has spent the last six years as a guard at that temple — steady work for a dwarf who knows steel and stone — until three nights gone, when three miners slipped into the old north workings and never came out.

Last night a fourth man crawled up alone, light-crazed, babbling about lights that walk, and was dead before the morning bell. At sunrise tomorrow the council will brick the entrance shut, missing miners or no. So the prior, Hessa, has asked what no one else would ask of him: go down before the bricks are laid, and bring back whoever can still be brought back.

It is late afternoon. Brakka stands at the mouth of the old north workings, where the air that comes out of the dark is cold and carries a smell of rust and wet stone. The temple lantern hangs heavy and unlit from his hand; a breath of mountain wind tugs at it. A few yards behind him the day's last light still lies over the trampled flat ground in front of the old timbers shoring up the opening. Past those timbers the tunnel runs away into blackness.

What does he do?


>>>>>> round 1, the player: I light the temple lantern and check my gear before heading in: shield strapped tight, weapon loose in its sheath. Before I step past the timbers, I crouch at the mouth and look over the trampled ground for tracks, noting which way the three miners' boots went and whether anything else came out.

  [dice Brakka's Wisdom (Survival) check to read the tracks at the mine mouth: 1d20+2 = 1d20 [11] + 2 = 13]
  [time: timer Hooded lantern (Brakka's): 6 hours]

The lantern catches with a hiss and settles to a steady pool of yellow light that Brakka keeps hooded low while he works. Gear is checked in the old way, quickly and without thought: shield cinched to his forearm, the longsword loose enough in its sheath to come free in one pull.

Crouched at the mouth, he reads the ground. The trampled flat has been churned by half the town these three days, but the fresher marks cut through it. Three sets of boots, dwarf-made and heavy-soled, went in side by side, walking close together the way men walk into a mine they know. None of them comes back out.

One set does come out, and it is ugly to read: the same kind of boot, but the stride breaks apart after a few yards — wide, lurching steps, then the marks of a man going to his knees once, twice, and a long ragged drag where he crawled part of the way. The fourth man, alone, and already going.

And beneath even that, faint in the hail of overlapping prints, there is something else. Not boots. A set of bare, smooth impressions, like soft feet or the flattened prints of something that weighs almost nothing, spaced strangely wide. They come out of the dark mouth and then stop short of it, as if whatever left them turned back. Brakka cannot make out what made them, only that they are not human, and not dwarf.

The tunnel past the timbers waits, black and cold, the smell of wet stone rolling out of it.

What does he do?


>>>>>> round 2, the player: I make a note of those strange bare prints and mutter a quick word to the Anvil Father. Then I unhood the lantern a little, shield up and sword hand ready, and step past the timbers, moving slowly and watching the floor, walls, and ceiling as I go.

