He murmurs the old invocation under his breath — "Anvil an' forge, hold true" — and steps past the timbers with the lantern unhooded enough to throw a moving island of light down the passage.

The tunnel runs gently down and bends away north. It is an old working: the floor is packed dirt and splintered rock chips, the walls rough-hewn and beaded with wet, and every few yards a wooden prop stands, some straight, most leaning, the dust thick on their shoulders. A ribbon of rusted rail lies half-buried down the left side where ore carts once ran. The air is cold and still, and it smells of water and old iron, and faintly, underneath, of something turned.

Brakka goes slow, lantern sweeping floor to ceiling. Nothing moves but his own shadow pacing along the wall. The boot tracks stay with him, three sets in and one broken set out, the strange bare prints gone into the dark ahead or turned aside where he cannot say.

Ten minutes down, the passage widens and the light finds what it was bound to find: the old seal. A wall of mortared stone is set across the tunnel, bricks the colour of old ash. A generation's dust lies over it — except at the right side, where a hole has been broken through, rough and low, the bricks tumbled inward across the floor. The miners' tracks go straight into it. So does the drag-mark of the man who came back alone.

The cold air pours out through the hole like a held breath.

What does he do?


>>>>>> round 3, the player: I kneel at the breach and check the tumbled bricks: did they fall inward or get pushed from this side, and is there any sign of what broke through? Then, lantern low and shield up, I duck through the hole and move carefully on.

  [dice Brakka's Intelligence (Investigation) check to read how the seal was broken, DC 12: 1d20-1 = 1d20 [20] - 1 = 19 (natural 20)]
