He keeps low, shield toward the far passage, and tilts the waterskin to Torvin's cracked lips. "Slow sips," he says. "I'm Brakka, from Prior Hessa's temple." The dwarf drinks in short, trembling pulls, water running down the dust on his chin, and some colour of life crawls back into the act even if not into his grey face.

"Well met, Brakka," Torvin rasps. "Heard your steel all the way from the bend. Thought — thought you were them." He whispers the rest fast, eyes sliding to the dark as he speaks.

"The other two? Bredi and Dulgan. We cut through the seal because something knocked on the far side of it, three nights running — a slow, patient knock, like a man too weak to hammer. We'd have sworn there was someone alive in here, trapped since the sealing, living off the drips." He shakes his head. "There was no one. There was only the dark, and the lights."

"The lights walk no road," he goes on. "Cold lights, small and blue as frost. They'll hang at the edge of what you can see, and when you look straight at them they turn away. That's not the danger, though. When a light goes out — that's when the cold comes. It comes up out of the dark and it lays its hands on you, and it draws the warmth clean out — I saw it on Bredi. He went grey, here." He touches his own breast. "And then he just... turned, and walked off after the light with Dulgan after him, calling, and neither of them came back through the far passage." 

His gaze finds the waterskin, and his voice drops. "I couldn't follow. The props gave under me the first day; tore my shoulder open on the rock. I've sat here knocking since, so the others could find me back. The fourth one — the one who ran — he'd been touched too. Was he... did he make it out?"

The far passage lies in blackness. The light has not returned.

What does he do?


>>>>>> round 7, the player: I tell him gently, "He crawled out, Torvin, but he died before morning. The council bricks up the entrance at sunrise, so we've got until then." I bind his shoulder with what I carry and ask, "Can you walk if I help you, or should I get you to the surface first? And where does that far passage lead?"

  [time: 2 minutes later: Day 1, 5:22 PM]

"He crawled out, Torvin," Brakka says gently. "But he was gone before morning. I'm sorry." 

Torvin's jaw works for a long moment, then he nods once and looks away into the dark. "Aye. Well. I'll not pretend he had a poor death of it, coming up into the light." His voice turns dry as the dust. "You said the council bricks it shut at sunrise. Then there's no more 'until then' than that."

Brakka lays him back against the cart and binds the shoulder with strips of cloth, snug, the way a temple guard learns to do in a pinch. Torvin sets his teeth but says nothing. When it's done he rolls the shoulder once, testing, and grunts.

"I can walk," he says. "Give me your arm to lean and the wall to mind, and I'll make a slow pace of it. But I'll not have you carry me up and leave Bredi and Dulgan to the cold." He grips the pick-haft and uses it to sit straighter. "That far passage runs a long drift down to the lowest gallery — the deep north seam. It's where the knocking was loudest the whole time, and where the lights were thickest, and it's the way Bredi walked off, with Dulgan after him. I can show you. But you'd best know: down there the dark don't stay put in the corners the way it does up here."

What does he do?


>>>>>> round 8, the player: "Then we go together, but slow, and you tell me every landmark before we reach it." I help him to his feet, keep him on my shield-free side, and say, "Torvin, I've got your tally chip, found it at the seal. If we see a light go out, you say so first and we back off, and I'll hold the front with the lantern."

  [time: 20 minutes later: Day 1, 5:42 PM]
