He eases Torvin down against the wall; the man's fist closes tight around his chip and he nods, keeping his eyes on the dark as bidden.

Brakka hoods the lantern to a sliver and creeps forward. But chain mail is chain mail: a single link rasps against the shield's rim, small as a fly in the stone hush, and the sound goes out ahead of him and does not come back.

At the mouth the drift opens into the lowest gallery — a wide, low hall roofed by square-cut stone pillars, the floor strewn with rotted timber and a pushed-over ore cart. And there is the knocker. Against the centre pillar sits a figure, and the slow rhythm comes from him — a pick-haft rising and falling, knock, and a pause, and knock.

At the sound of Brakka's armor the knocking stops. The figure's head turns, very slowly, toward the mouth. It is a dwarf in miner's gear, grey with dust — grey as a man gone three days past warmth, the colour of it settled deep into the skin and the rail of his arms. When his face comes round, the faint lantern-sliver finds his eyes, and they shine with the same flat blue as the lights. He does not rise. He only watches.

And in the far corners, two of the cold lights that had been turning away now drift inward instead, slow and soundless, toward Brakka.

From the shadows behind him comes Torvin's quick, ragged whisper: "That's Dulgan. Anvil keep us, Brakka — that's Dulgan, and he's not right."

What does he do?


>>>>>> round 10, the player: I keep my shield up and speak low and steady. "Dulgan! It's Brakka, from Hessa's temple. We've come to take you home." I open the lantern a little to show my face and the temple's mark, and hold my ground at the mouth, ready to back up to Torvin if the lights come closer.

