He opens the lantern full, and the warmth of it leaps outward; the two lights flinch back from the widening circle like things shrinking from a brand, and hang again at the new rim, circling.

Keeping his back to the drift mouth and Torvin behind him, Brakka steps toward Dulgan, shield up. The air around the kneeling dwarf is a well of cold the lantern's warmth cannot reach — it pools along his arms, thin as frost. As Brakka stops a pace off, Dulgan's grey hand lifts from the pick and reaches toward him, not fast, the way a man reaches for a warmth he has half forgotten, and his face pulls back into itself with fear.

"The light wanted my warmth," he whispers, the words his own. "That's all it ever wanted. It draws it out and leaves the shell, and the shell keeps knocking so the next warm thing will come down." His hand closes at his chest. "Bredi — it walked him down to the old fall. The north seam, where it all gave way. That's where the dead are thickest, where they bricked 'em in and never said a word over 'em." His eyes, briefly clear, find Brakka's face. "They've been waiting for their rest down there. And they'll take it warm, if no one will give it to 'em by prayer."

The clarity wears thin; his hand drifts back toward the pick-haft. "You're of the temple," he says, half question and half plea. "You could lay 'em down proper. Give 'em over to the Anvil Father." Then the blue rises up over his eyes again, and the slow knock begins once more.

Beyond the lights, the far end of the gallery drops away into a deeper black — the way down to the old fall.

What does he do?


>>>>>> round 12, the player: "Stay with me, Dulgan, I'll lay them down proper, I swear it by the Anvil Father." I take the temple's holy symbol in my free hand and speak a short prayer of rest over him, then try to guide him gently to his feet and back toward Torvin, keeping my lantern between us and those two lights.

